[p127] 3.5 Pricing Analysis and Scenario
[p127] Evaluation
[p127] 
[p128]                5. PRICING ANALYSIS AND SCENARIO EVALUATION
[p128] The MVP must provide a structured analytical environment for evaluating the financial and
[p128] commercial implications of pricing work before it is submitted for decision.
[p128] 
[p128] The purpose of this capability is not advanced optimisation. It is to ensure that users can
[p128] understand the likely effect of a proposed pricing action using available organisational data,
[p128] explicit assumptions and comparable scenarios.
[p128] 
[p128]

[p128]       5.1.    ANALYSIS-001 Initiative-Level Analysis
[p128] 
[p128] 
[p128] Every Pricing Initiative must provide an analysis area connected to:
[p128] 
[p128]     ● the affected pricing scope;
[p128]     ● current pricing data;
[p128]     ● relevant cost records and the applicable unit cost selected or assumed for the
[p128]         analysis;
[p128]     ● pricing logic;
[p128]     ● assumptions;
[p128]     ● scenarios;
[p128]     ● applicable guardrails.
[p128] 
[p128] Analysis must remain part of the Pricing Initiative rather than exist as a disconnected
[p128] calculator.
[p128] 
[p128]

[p128]       5.2.    ANALYSIS-002 Deterministic Pricing Calculation Coverage
[p128] 
[p128] 
[p128] Where the required inputs are available, the MVP must provide deterministic calculation
[p128] support for the quantitative pricing actions defined in the Pricing Builder.
[p128] 
[p128] The analytical calculation layer must support, where applicable:
[p128] 
[p128]     ● direct entry of a proposed selling price;
[p128]     ● percentage increase or decrease from a defined baseline price;
[p128]     ● absolute-value increase or decrease from a defined baseline price;
[p128]     ● derivation of a selling price from a target gross-margin percentage;
[p128]     ● derivation of a selling price from a target markup percentage;
[p128]     ● promotional or discount pricing expressed as a percentage reduction;
[p128] 
[p129]    ● promotional or discount pricing expressed as an absolute-value reduction;
[p129]    ● markdown pricing expressed through a supported percentage or absolute-value
[p129]          adjustment;
[p129]    ● calculation of the resulting selling price from supported structured pricing logic where
[p129]          all required inputs and operators are deterministically evaluable.
[p129] 
[p129] For the current and resulting proposed pricing position, the MVP must calculate or preserve,
[p129] where applicable:
[p129] 
[p129]    ● current selling price;
[p129]    ● proposed selling price;
[p129]    ● absolute price change;
[p129]    ● percentage price change;
[p129]    ● applicable unit cost;
[p129]    ● gross profit per unit;
[p129]    ● gross margin percentage;
[p129]    ● markup percentage.
[p129] 
[p129] A pricing action must not be presented as deterministically calculated where the system
[p129] does not have sufficient inputs or a supported calculation method to derive its result.
[p129] 
[p129] Pricing approaches that contain qualitative judgement, unsupported custom logic or
[p129] conditions that cannot be deterministically evaluated may still be represented within the
[p129] Pricing Initiative, but the system must distinguish those elements from calculated quantitative
[p129] outputs.
[p129] 
[p129] The same calculation definitions must be used consistently across the Pricing Builder
[p129] preview, pricing analysis, Scenarios, Proposal preparation and guardrail evaluation.
[p129] 
[p129] AI must not independently define or alter these deterministic calculation methods.
[p129] 
[p129]

[p135]       5.8.    ANALYSIS-008 Scenario Creation
[p135] 
[p135] 
[p135] Authorised users must be able to create multiple pricing scenarios within an initiative.
[p135] 
[p135] A scenario represents an alternative pricing approach under consideration.
[p135] 
[p135] Each scenario should contain, where relevant:
[p135] 
[p135]    ● name;
[p135]    ● pricing action;
[p135]    ● proposed price or pricing logic;
[p135]    ● assumptions;
[p135]    ● affected scope;
[p135]    ● calculated outputs;
[p135]    ● guardrail status;
[p135]    ● creator;
[p135]    ● timestamp.
[p135]

[p135]     5.10.     ANALYSIS-010 Scenario Comparison
[p135] 
[p135] 
[p135] Users must be able to compare scenarios using relevant measures.
[p135] 
[p135] Where inputs are available, comparison should include:
[p135] 
[p136]    ● proposed price;
[p136]    ● price change;
[p136]    ● gross profit;
[p136]    ● gross margin;
[p136]    ● markup;
[p136]    ● applicable guardrail status;
[p136]    ● material assumptions.
[p136] 
[p136] Scenario comparison must make trade-offs visible rather than presenting only a
[p136] recommended option.
[p136] 
[p136]

[p140]     5.19.     ANALYSIS-019 Qualitative Decision Inputs
[p140] 
[p140] 
[p140] The analysis area must be able to reference qualitative decision inputs that materially affect
[p140] interpretation of pricing work but are not deterministic calculation outputs.
[p140] 
[p141] Qualitative decision inputs may originate from:
[p141] 
[p141]    ● structured Pricing Context governed by Section 3.2;
[p141]    ● Evidence governed by Section 3.7;
[p141]    ● explicit Assumptions governed by ANALYSIS-017;
[p141]    ● attributable user-authored analytical interpretation.
[p141] 
[p141] Relevant examples may include:
[p141] 
[p141]    ● competitor observations;
[p141]    ● customer or sales feedback;
[p141]    ● supplier information;
[p141]    ● market conditions;
[p141]    ● operational constraints;
[p141]    ● commercial judgement.
[p141] 
[p141] The analytical layer must preserve the originating information type rather than creating a
[p141] separate competing qualitative-context object.
[p141] 
[p141] Analysis must not:
[p141] 
[p141]    ● represent Evidence as verified structured organisational data;
[p141]    ● represent an Assumption as a verified fact;
[p141]    ● represent user interpretation as Evidence;
[p141]    ● represent AI interpretation as recorded organisational fact;
[p141]    ● convert a qualitative input into a deterministic calculation output merely because it
[p141]        influenced the analysis.
[p141] 
[p141] Where a qualitative decision input materially supports a submitted Pricing Proposal, the
[p141] Proposal and historical record must preserve sufficient reference to its originating
[p141] information type and relevant source or version context.
[p141] 
[p141]

[p141]     5.20.     ANALYSIS-020 AI-Assisted Analysis
[p141] 
[p141] 
[p141] Users must be able to invoke AI to assist in interpreting the available pricing analysis.
[p141] 
[p141] AI may:
[p141] 
[p141]    ● explain calculations;
[p141]    ● summarise differences between scenarios;
[p141] 
[p142]    ● identify material trade-offs;
[p142]    ● identify missing information;
[p142]    ● surface inconsistencies;
[p142]    ● explain guardrail breaches;
[p142]    ● highlight potentially consequential assumptions;
[p142]    ● help users formulate additional scenarios.
[p142] 
[p142] AI-generated interpretation must not alter underlying calculations.
[p142] 
[p142]

[p142]      5.22.    ANALYSIS-022 Calculation Traceability
[p142] 
[p142] 
[p142] Users must be able to understand and reproduce the basis of consequential deterministic
[p142] pricing calculations.
[p142] 
[p142] For each material calculated output, the MVP must preserve enough calculation context to
[p142] determine, where applicable:
[p142] 
[p142]    ● pricing action;
[p142]    ● affected pricing object or scope;
[p142]    ● calculation baseline;
[p142]    ● source input values;
[p142]    ● source of those values;
[p142]    ● explicitly assumed values;
[p142]    ● calculation parameter or adjustment value;
[p142]    ● operator;
[p142]    ● formula or deterministic calculation method used;
[p142]    ● resulting unrounded calculated value;
[p142]    ● explicitly applied rounding or final-value adjustment, where applicable;
[p142]    ● resulting proposed value used by subsequent pricing work.
[p142] 
[p143] A calculated output must remain distinguishable from a directly entered value, explicit
[p143] assumption or manually determined commercial value.
[p143] 
[p143] Calculation traceability must remain consistent across the Pricing Builder, analysis,
[p143] Scenarios, Proposal preparation and guardrail evaluation.
[p143] 
[p143] Pricing analysis must therefore be reproducible from the preserved decision-relevant pricing
[p143] context.
[p143] 
[p143]

[p145]     5.27.     ANALYSIS-027 Analysis Snapshot
[p145] 
[p145] 
[p145] When a Pricing Proposal is submitted, the decision-relevant analysis supporting that
[p145] Proposal version must be preserved.
[p145] 
[p145] Where cost-dependent calculations form part of the analysis, the preserved analysis must
[p145] retain, where applicable:
[p145] 
[p145]    ● the Applicable Unit Cost used;
[p145]    ● cost basis;
[p145]    ● currency;
[p145]    ● applicable unit of measure;
[p145]    ● source Cost Record or explicit cost assumption;
[p145]    ● relevant effective context;
[p145]    ● calculated financial outputs dependent on that cost.
[p145] 
[p145] Subsequent changes to:
[p145] 
[p145]    ● Cost Records;
[p145]    ● selection of the Applicable Unit Cost;
[p145]    ● cost basis;
[p145]    ● current prices;
[p145]    ● assumptions;
[p145]    ● Scenarios;
[p145]    ● underlying workspace data
[p145] 
[p145] must not silently modify, recalculate or reinterpret the analysis associated with an already
[p145] submitted Proposal version.
[p145] 
[p146] Where an explicit assumed Applicable Unit Cost formed part of the submitted analysis, that
[p146] value must remain identifiable as an assumption rather than verified organisational cost
[p146] data.
[p146] 
[p146] Historical analysis must continue to reflect the exact cost context and other decision-relevant
[p146] inputs used when the Proposal version was submitted.
